  {
    "episode_id": 72683,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72683.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "I'm very interested in climate too, and I think all of the committee is every person as I think about them in my mind. I think the difference is I'd rather use the carrot rather than the stick. And so whatever we do, as far as I'm concerned, we'll work towards being voluntary, give incentives, things like that. Our farmers do a great job now. Additional research to give them more tools. The Growing Climate Solutions Act, which is going to give them more toolbox. strictly voluntary, participating in a non-governmental, a private sector, carbon market, if they choose to do so, all of those kind of things. But I think that's the difference is I'm very concerned about trying to hook climate to crop insurance or something like that. And we hear talk of individuals wanting to do that at USDA. So we're not going to do that. You know, we simply, and I'm working really hard to visit with everybody I can in agriculture. I think these are the kind of things that we need to stick together on. And if we do that, agriculture is a mighty force. We saw that with what they did with the, when they tried to get rid of stepped up basis and the 1031 land exchange, you know, they said no. And as a result, the administration had to back off. We can be very, very proud of that. What I don't want is Washington telling the farmer what to plant, when to plan it, how to plan it, and all those kind of things. And there are some in Washington that would very much like to do that. And that's really what about water, what waters of the US is in programs like that. That's all about controlling land use. So we're going to work really hard to make sure that those things don't happen.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
