    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72683.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think they need to listen to myself and my constituents. And then again, just farmers and ranchers, really, just the general population throughout the country. I think the Constitution is very clear, navigable waters. The court has extended that. But not to the point that I think under the Obama administration's interpretation of that, something like 97% of the waters in Arkansas were recovered. That's hardly navigable water. So our founders were very careful in making sure that was all about interstate commerce. Those were the highways of the day. But they simply don't have the authority to do that. So this is all about land control coming out of Washington. They want it to be such that if you've got a drainage ditch on your property that you have to ask them what you can do with your property, all of those kind of things. The other problem is that simply you can't get the answer to that question. You know, sometimes for months, years, whatever. So this is a huge issue. It's a huge issue for our farm communities and also our developers and really everyone. You know, you decide you're going to add on to your house and you've got a drainage ditch out back. You've got to get the EPA or whoever to come out and give you permission. So we've got to use some common sense. We've got to use the science before it and then very importantly We have to adhere to the Constitution. They don't want to hear that But but that's the reality. So I think ultimately the courts are going to be on on our side",
